                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1086e06e-9cc5-40e8-a30d-655c0eb5afbf",
                        "source_entry_id": "67dce4e1-4f65-389e-b5fa-2f85cf3003f8",
                        "source_sense_id": "1d0453ce-f5c1-3ee5-8929-928bdcc0a4e8",
                        "target_lang_word": "antisymmetric",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "antisymmetric",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "82abcf7f-bb58-3eb0-8306-cdf9720df638",
                                        "entry_id": "33c9a3e7-f308-3da2-aae7-5eef607b556e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Having the property that, for any two distinct elements of S, at least one is not related to the other via R; equivalently, having the property that, for any x, y ∈ S, if both xRy and yRx then x=y."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0adcbb02-3222-440f-aa5c-67293fc5fbfb",
                                                "sense_id": "82abcf7f-bb58-3eb0-8306-cdf9720df638",
                                                "text": "1987, David C. Buchthal, Douglas E. Cameron, Modern Abstract Algebra, Prindle, Weber & Schmidt, page 479,\nThe standard example for an antisymmetric relation is the relation less than or equal to on the real number system."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3652d60e-ca6f-4e1e-8d9f-493a5db6451e",
                                                "sense_id": "82abcf7f-bb58-3eb0-8306-cdf9720df638",
                                                "text": "(i) The identity relation on a set A is an antisymmetric relation.\n(ii) Let R be a relation on the set N of natural numbers defined by\n x R y #92;Leftrightarrow 'x divides y' for all x, y ∈ N.\nThis relation is an antisymmetric relation on N."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0731b5da-8a62-313c-bb0d-c00f9ecb7311",
                                        "entry_id": "33c9a3e7-f308-3da2-aae7-5eef607b556e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Whose sign changes on the application of a matrix transpose or some generalisation thereof:",
                                            "Whose transpose equals its negative (i.e., Mᵀ = −M);"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2f706e13-260d-4b5b-835f-d8eb7c683477",
                                                "sense_id": "0731b5da-8a62-313c-bb0d-c00f9ecb7311",
                                                "text": "The eigenvalues of an antisymmetric matrix are all purely imaginary numbers, and occur as conjugate pairs, #43;iw and -iw. As a corollary it follows that an antisymmetric matrix of odd order necessarily has one eigenvalue equal to zero; antisymmetric matrices of odd order are singular."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6b310cb0-37ce-35a3-8a7e-86f27e39246a",
                                        "entry_id": "33c9a3e7-f308-3da2-aae7-5eef607b556e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Whose sign changes on the application of a matrix transpose or some generalisation thereof:",
                                            "That changes sign when any two indices are interchanged (e.g., Tᵢⱼₖ = -Tⱼᵢₖ);"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cbe00e4f-eb23-43bd-8472-4ccef405645c",
                                                "sense_id": "6b310cb0-37ce-35a3-8a7e-86f27e39246a",
                                                "text": "Notice that the tensors defined by:\n #92;textstyleT#95;S#92;equiv#92;frac#123;1#125;#123;2#125;(T#43;Tᵀ), #92;textstyleT#95;A#92;equiv#92;frac#123;1#125;#123;2#125;(T-Tᵀ), (3.47)\nare the symmetric and antisymmetric parts, respectively; they are known as the symmetric and antisymmetric parts of T."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8ee85e36-d213-3801-b78b-ca4b613d0615",
                                        "entry_id": "33c9a3e7-f308-3da2-aae7-5eef607b556e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Whose sign changes on the application of a matrix transpose or some generalisation thereof:",
                                            "For which B(w,v) = -B(v,w)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "15b1f576-0184-4134-be3c-85a9055e355a",
                                                "sense_id": "8ee85e36-d213-3801-b78b-ca4b613d0615",
                                                "text": "Antisymmetric bilinear forms and wedge products are defined exactly as above, only now they are functions from #92;Rⁿ#92;times#92;Rⁿ to #92;R.[…]\nExercise 21 Show that every antisymmetric bilinear form on #92;R³ is a wedge product of two covectors."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
